00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.520
Last January, the inflation rate in the United States

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.280
reached a new record level of 7.9%,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.440
which is the highest and fastest rate of inflation in America in 40 years,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.960
specifically from February 1982,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.120
when the inflation rate at that time reached 7.6%

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.360
It is assumed that the target inflation rate will be Between 1% - 2% only.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:24.240
This means that there is a major imbalance in the American economy,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.120
and here comes the role of the US Federal Reserve, which can address this imbalance through interest rates.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:36.720
On Wednesday evening, the US Federal Reserve raised interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point by 0.25%,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.720
that is the first time the Fed raises interest rates in 4 years, in December 2018.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.280
This increase was actually what we expected in March in the episode of the 2022 economic forecasts.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.680
It was published last month, and it will give you a clear view of many things that will most likely happen,

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:54.880
You can see it from here

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:09.520
The Fed will not stop at this increase only, officials expect that interest rates will reach 1.75% by the end of the current year 2022.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.560
This means that there are about 6 more increases in the way,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.240
assuming that the Fed will move in quarter-point increments as it did two days ago.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:26.520
There are expectations that the increase in interest rates will continue in 2023 as well.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:33.840
Here, of course, a crucial question arises: What is the effect of raising US interest rates on us here in the Arab world?

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.080
Of course, the effect will be different from one country to another, according to a lot of data related to each economy alone

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.480
In summary, we can clarify some of the effects as follows

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:50.640
The most direct impact that will affect each and every arabic monetary system is the expected raise in the dollar exchange rate, in comparison with Arab currencies,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.240
specifically those of countries that allow an exchange rate with a type of flexibility.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:02.560
That is why we find that most Arab central banks are also raising interest rates after the US Federal decision,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.360
in order to reduce financial burdens on its currency and markets.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.960
The financial burden of these economies will even get more complicated if they are suffering several dollar debts.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.680
The depreciation of its local currency against the dollar will mount its obligations,

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.320
in other words, they will be vulnerable to default on its debts.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:26.040
Even when these countries resort to foreign external borrowing, the cost will be high,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:33.560
so, this will make their chances of getting a loan pretty difficult. Otherwise, they must raise interest rates to higher levels in order to be able to attract creditors,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:38.280
and in the end this increases their debt levels. So, the question is: what is the volume of Arab countries debt?

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.680
In a recent study, the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Western Asia (ESCWA),

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.800
last October 2021, it warned of the unprecedented increase in the public debt in the Arab countries,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.840
and that it reached 1.4 trillion dollars in 2020,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.320
which represents about 60% of the gross domestic product of the Arab region.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:05.440
Another concern related to the Fed's raising of the interest rate is the exit of foreign investments in debt instruments in the Arab markets

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.400
As a result of the Federal Reserve raising interest rates,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.640
the US market becomes more attractive to international investors

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.680
Thus investors layover their business in the developing and emerging markets that experience many risks

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:22.400
to flee to the US market lusting for the high interest rate with very few risks.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.400
This creates heavy financial burdens on the markets of countries that depend on foreign currencies,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:33.400
as they will just go away with the slightest changes in profit rates, leaving a gap that can not be ridged in these economies.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:40.680
The decline in the value of local currencies against the dollar, the high cost of borrowing from abroad and the exit of foreign capital,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:46.880
will lead to an increase in the deficit of local budgets, consequently, social spending and support programs will be reduced.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:54.280
This will lead to a decline in economic growth, an increase in unemployment rates and a deterioration in living standards.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:08:02.760
Another hazardous effect of raising US interest rates and consequently the depreciation of the local currency against the dollar is: imported inflation.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.360
This happens to countries that have a large deficit in the trade balance.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.240
they import more than they export this drives an increase in the cost of imports.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:19.520
On the other hand, the rate of the local currency decreases against the dollar will be a strong point in the export-dependent economies

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:26.840
because their exports will be cheaper in international markets and this is a competitive advantage that gives away to them to enter new markets.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:31.880
Raising interest rates in the United States will lead to a decline in the prices of gold all over the world.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:37.240
The reason is that investors who used to protect their investments by buying and keeping gold

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:42.400
(especially in this troubled time that the world is going through after the Russian Ukrainian war),

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:50.354
will begin to change their minds and dispense with gold to invest in US Treasury bonds as their benefit from the rise in interest rates,

00:08:50.354 --> 00:08:58.800
whether at the current level or the upcoming rises in interest rates, and this leads to an increase in the supply of gold in the world and thus a decrease in its price.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.867
The same negative effect will reach oil prices, although there are many factors involved in the oil price equation,

00:09:04.867 --> 00:09:09.680
such as Western sanctions on one of the largest oil producers in the world: Russia.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:17.400
Taking in consideration other factors that are being stabilized, raising interest rates in the United States leads to an increase in the dollar exchange rate.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:24.720
The dollar is the main currency used in oil trading, and this makes crude oil and petroleum products more expensive for

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:31.779
consumers who use other currencies, thus, affects the global demand for oil and its price drops may drop by time.

00:09:31.779 --> 00:09:39.600
However, as I said a while ago, there are many factors that control oil prices in particular other than The US Federal Reserve raising the interest rate,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:45.200
so cautions must be taken. In the end, we can generally say, that the effect of the Federal Reserve raising interest rates

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.680
will be negative on developing and emerging economies such as the Arab economies,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:55.522
and the only way for our Arab countries out of this crisis is through strengthening the foundations of their economies at all levels,

00:09:55.522 --> 00:10:01.520
in addition to reducing imports in exchange for focusing on the export-based economic model.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:09.800
As for individuals (or what we can do in such period of time), there is an upcoming episode in which we will focus on how we can act as individuals currently.

